{"uncertainty": 0.2, "outcome": "rtl_bug", "artifacts": [{"kind": "root_cause_analysis", "title": "uart_tx: a start pulse during a frame reloads the hold register", "summary": "Requirement [requirement:req-d10c4df532]. RTL under investigation: [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1] (uart_tx_reload_while_busy.v). Its header states the contract: 'A start pulse while a frame is in flight is ignored.'\n\nOBSERVED: [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e2] / [run:run-0002]: icarus built the design, reached $finish at 546000 and reported 1 of 4 checks failed. [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e1] / [run:run-0001]: VeriTriage classified the failure as unknown_failure at 30% confidence, with 'Likely RTL bug' as its top hypothesis. I treat that hypothesis as one candidate, not as the conclusion.\n\nDEFECT: Inside the always block, before the case statement, the RTL has an unconditional 'if (start) hold <= data;'. It executes in every state (START, DATA, STOP), not only in IDLE. The IDLE branch already loads hold on start, so this line is redundant there and wrong everywhere else.\n\nCAUSALITY: Suppose start is asserted while busy=1. On that clock edge hold is overwritten with the new data. The state machine keeps going, because only IDLE acts on start. Every data bit sent after the reload comes from the new byte, since 'tx <= hold[0]' and 'tx <= hold[bit_idx + 3'd1]' read hold live. The fault is the hold write at the cycle of the second start pulse. It only becomes visible later, when the corrupted bits reach tx, so the failing check marks visibility, not origin. The result is a mixed or wholly replaced frame. In the repro the second pulse lands during the start bit, so the whole payload is replaced (0xA5 becomes 0x3C).\n\nINCORRECTNESS: The header contract says a start pulse during a frame is ignored. A design that changes in-flight data is not ignoring it. The same defect corrupts any frame whenever start is asserted again before STOP completes. The data bits already sent stay as they were; the later ones change.\n\nSUGGESTED FIX (not applied here): delete the pre-case 'if (start) hold <= data;' and rely on the IDLE-branch load.\n\nRANKED HYPOTHESES AND HOW EACH IS SETTLED:\n1. rtl_bug (accepted). Refutation evidence would be: the repro tb/uart_tx_reload_repro_tb.v passing on [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1], or failing on RTL with the line removed. The platform's expect=fail gate checks the first. The scorer's clean-RTL run checks the second.\n2. testbench_bug (rejected). The expected values in my repro come from the header contract, not from the RTL's output: the first byte sent is 0xA5, the second start is ignored, and the frame is 8N1 LSB-first. The receiver is a UART-style sampler driven by the tx falling edge, so it does not depend on internal signal names. The sanity frame with no interference is expected to pass on both clean and buggy RTL. If that sanity check failed, it would point back to the testbench.\n3. infrastructure (rejected). [run:run-0002] shows a successful build, a deterministic $finish, and exactly one failing check out of four. A tool, license or build failure would not produce that.\n4. spec_ambiguity (rejected, with a residual caveat). The contract in [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1] states the in-flight behaviour explicitly. The caveat is that no separate specification document was among my inputs; the header comment is the only clause available.\n\nRESIDUAL UNCERTAINTY: The regression's own failing check is not described in the evidence. My mapping of the 1-of-4 failure in [run:run-0002] to this defect is therefore an inference from the RTL, not an observation."}], "files": [{"path": "tb/uart_tx_reload_repro_tb.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Self-checking repro: start pulse while busy must not change the in-flight byte", "summary": "Minimal repro for the defect in [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1], serving [requirement:req-d10c4df532].\n\nWhat it does:\n- Resets the design and sends 0xA5.\n- Two cycles later, during the start bit and while busy=1, pulses start with 0x3C.\n- Receives the frame with an edge-triggered mid-bit sampler and checks start bit, byte == 0xA5 and stop bit.\n- Checks that the ignored pulse launched no second frame.\n- Sends one clean 0x5A frame as a sanity check of the receiver.\n\nExpected values come from the header contract, not from RTL output.\n\nExpected results:\n- Buggy RTL: the byte check fails (0x3C received), giving 7 passed, 1 failed.\n- Clean RTL: 8 passed, 0 failed.\n\nIt prints exactly one 'NIRMAAN-SIM <nonce> <n> passed, <m> failed' line. A watchdog reports a failure if the frame never arrives.", "entry": "uart_tx_reload_repro_tb"}], "tool_runs": ["run-0001", "run-0002"], "claims": ["The testbench reads the nonce from plusarg NIRMAAN_NONCE, falling back to NONCE or nonce. The exact plusarg name is not stated in my inputs; if the platform uses another name, the verdict line will carry an empty nonce and the gate will reject it.", "On the RTL under investigation the repro is expected to report 7 passed, 1 failed. On RTL without the pre-case hold load it is expected to report 8 passed, 0 failed. Neither result has been run by me.", "The 1-of-4 failure in the regression is assumed to come from the same hold-reload defect. The failing check's name and values were not available to me."], "escalation": null, "notes": "run-0001 and run-0002 are triage-stage runs I relied on as evidence; I ran no tools myself. The platform runs the reproduction gate (simulator.run, expect=fail) over tb/uart_tx_reload_repro_tb.v with top uart_tx_reload_repro_tb. Independent review is still required before this decision can be accepted. The verdict line is built with $write per character so that leading NUL bytes of the nonce register are never printed."}
